**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["5B.2"]}

E0001 is the authority bridge only. It helps attribute economic appointments and cabinet-level control to the Hasina administration, but it does not by itself prove good or bad economic stewardship.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mr. Abdur Rouf Talukder Former Governor, Bangladesh Bank","publisher":"Bangladesh Bank","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.bb.org.bd/en/index.php/about/former_governor/abdur_rouf","claim":"Abdur Rouf Talukder joined as the 12th governor of Bangladesh Bank on 12 July 2022 after a long finance-ministry career; the bio says he contributed to budget reform, macroeconomic management, and IT in public financial management.","locator":"bio paragraphs identifying his appointment and prior roles","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official biography page from the central bank, with direct appointment and career details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 appointment and capacity context","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. The page does not show Sheikh Hasina ordering the appointment, but it documents the government-selected central-bank leadership available to her administration.","contrary_evidence":["The same bio shows a senior finance-ministry insider, so it supports competence but also suggests closeness to the executive rather than institutional independence."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"The IMF said Bangladesh entered the pandemic with macroeconomic stability, announced support packages worth Tk 1.9 trillion by curtailing non-priority spending, and had macro policies that kept inflation stable, debt-to-GDP low, and external buffers adequate.","locator":"paragraphs 239-245","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF country assessment with concrete macro indicators and policy response description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"baseline and early-2022 macro context","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina administration's economic policy, not to Hasina personally; the source evaluates government and central-bank actions.","contrary_evidence":["The same report warns that FY22 inflation would rise to 5.9 percent and the current account deficit would widen, so it is not an unalloyed success story."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"IMF directors urged Bangladesh to monitor inflation closely, normalize monetary policy, gradually increase exchange-rate flexibility, safeguard reserves, strengthen bank regulation and supervision, improve corporate governance, and address corruption.","locator":"Executive Board Assessment paragraphs 249-253","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF Board summary with explicit reform prescriptions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 policy setting","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. It reflects the policy environment and the government's available choices rather than a direct personal act by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The report also acknowledges the government's rapid pandemic response and fiscal prudence, so the record is mixed rather than purely negative."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Reaches Staff-Level Agreement with Bangladesh on the Extended Credit Facility/Extended Fund Facility and the Resilience and Sustainability Facility","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/08/pr22375-bangladesh","claim":"The IMF said the new program aimed to restore macroeconomic stability, protect the vulnerable, create fiscal space, contain inflation, modernize the monetary policy framework, strengthen the financial sector, improve the business climate, and build climate resilience.","locator":"paragraphs 243-259","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF mission statement with detailed policy objectives and rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 crisis response","ruler_attribution":"Indirect, as a government-level reform program negotiated with the Hasina administration rather than a personal statement by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same press release says the economy had been hit by a sharp widening of the current account deficit, rapid reserve decline, rising inflation, and slowing growth."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Helps Bangladesh Build Economic Resilience","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/04/19/world-bank-helps-bangladesh-build-economic-resilience","claim":"The World Bank said its $250 million financing would help Bangladesh build a stronger fiscal and financial sector, streamline the bank recovery framework, and support a legislative framework on payments to create a more efficient financial system.","locator":"opening paragraphs 24-25","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release on a Bangladesh policy credit with specific reform content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 policy implementation","ruler_attribution":"Government-level policy action under the Hasina administration; the source does not isolate personal direction by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The financing was designed to address resilience gaps, which implies the existing fiscal and financial systems still needed strengthening."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strong Trade Competitiveness, Financial Sector, and Well-functioning Cities are Critical for Sustained Growth in Bangladesh: World Bank","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/09/29/strong-trade-competitiveness-financial-sector-and-well-functioning-cities-are-critical-for-sustained-growth-in-banglades","claim":"The World Bank said Bangladesh needed a strong reform agenda to sustain growth, especially to address trade competitiveness erosion, financial-sector vulnerabilities, orderly urbanization, digital development, climate change, private-sector financing, and export diversification.","locator":"opening paragraphs 24-36","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank country press release summarizing a country economic memorandum.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 structural reform context","ruler_attribution":"This is a country-level reform assessment under the Hasina administration, not a direct ruler statement.","contrary_evidence":["The same piece credits Bangladesh with remarkable development progress and very fast growth over the previous decade."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank-Funded Project Drives Public Procurement Reform in Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-08-04","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2022/08/04/world-bank-funded-project-drives-public-procurement-reform-in-bangladesh","claim":"The e-GP portal brought public procurement online, cut procurement costs by about 7 percent, reduced tender time from 100 days to 57 days, processed 80 percent of public procurement expenditure in FY21, and improved integrity by reducing external influences and preserving audit trails.","locator":"Synopsis and Results sections, lines 69-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank results page with concrete implementation and outcome metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective but highly relevant to 2022 governance conditions","ruler_attribution":"Government-level reform implementation under the Hasina administration; the result page does not prove personal oversight by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The need to strengthen procurement integrity implies prior corruption and influence risks were material."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Budget Speech 2022-2023","publisher":"Ministry of Finance, Government of Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://bangladeshcustoms.gov.bd/budget/National_Budget_Speech_FY2022-23_English_Version.pdf","claim":"The FY2022-23 budget speech made inflation control, a competitive exchange rate, continued economic development, employment generation, and emergency support for low-income people explicit priorities, including a Tk 5,000 crore fund for shocks from natural disasters or other crises.","locator":"Chapter Seven and the low-income support section, lines 894-901 and 1237-1242","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government budget speech with explicit policy priorities and allocations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 budget and policy setting","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. The document is the finance minister's budget speech for the Hasina government; it reflects administration priorities, not a direct personal statement by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is aspirational and does not itself prove successful execution."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Budget Speech 2022-2023","publisher":"Ministry of Finance, Government of Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://bangladeshcustoms.gov.bd/budget/National_Budget_Speech_FY2022-23_English_Version.pdf","claim":"The budget allocated Tk 183,425 crore to social infrastructure, Tk 31,761 crore to primary and mass education, Tk 29,282 crore to health, Tk 44,690 crore to local government and rural development, and Tk 4,290 crore to women and children affairs; it also said social safety-net coverage had reached 29 percent of households and allocations had risen almost eight-fold since FY2008-09.","locator":"Chapter Six and social-safety-net section, lines 1028-1033, 1799-1801, 5651-5654, 2403-2406, and 2515-2519","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary budget text with explicit sectoral allocations and social-protection targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 budget and distribution policy","ruler_attribution":"Indirect government policy under the Hasina administration; the speech itself is by the finance minister, not Hasina personally.","contrary_evidence":["The speech reports planned rather than audited outcomes, so implementation remains to be verified."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPI 2022: Disappointing global and Bangladesh performances","publisher":"Transparency International Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-02-01","url":"https://www.ti-bangladesh.org/en/articles/commentary/6630","claim":"TIB said Bangladesh scored 25 on the CPI 2022, ranked 147th, and that the lack of visible action over the previous decade and the growth of impunity and political influence over accountability institutions had worsened corruption control.","locator":"paragraphs 41-50","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National TI chapter summarizing the CPI results and providing Bangladesh-specific interpretation with concrete score/rank data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 corruption conditions","ruler_attribution":"Country-level anti-corruption performance under the Hasina administration; the report does not isolate individual personal decisions.","contrary_evidence":["TIB explicitly notes the government's repeated anti-corruption pledges, so the source mixes stated intent with poor implementation."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Household Expenditure Survey 2022: Families Spend 42% of Income on Food | BBS Report","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2023-12-20","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/xjj8jgjvnj","claim":"The HIES 2022 report found that families spent about 42 percent of monthly income on food and only Tk 923 on average was left as savings, indicating substantial household budget pressure.","locator":"Sector-wise expenditure section and the opening paragraphs, lines 41-46 and 53-55","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable local reporting of the official BBS survey, but the underlying survey report is more authoritative than the news article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 household conditions","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome under the Hasina administration; not direct proof of ruler action or omission.","contrary_evidence":["The survey is a 2022 snapshot and does not by itself separate government policy from imported inflation or private consumption shocks."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Total labour force in the country is 73.41m","publisher":"BSS News","publication_date":"2023-03-29","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news-flash/118166","claim":"The provisional Labour Force Survey 2022 said Bangladesh's total labour force reached 73.41 million and the unemployment rate fell to 3.6 percent from 4.2 percent in 2016-17.","locator":"paragraphs 39-45 and 85-87","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official news service reporting BBS provisional survey results; useful for labor-market context but still one-step removed from the underlying report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 labor-market conditions","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome under the administration, not direct evidence of a specific Hasina policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The piece reports labor-force expansion more than job quality, so it does not settle underemployment or real-wage questions."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh’s current account deficit widens as imports surge","publisher":"Prothom Alo / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/business/local/3jnadd7dk3","claim":"Reuters reported that Bangladesh's current account deficit widened to 4.2 percent of GDP in FY22, foreign-exchange reserves fell below $40 billion, and the government sought IMF support after import costs surged and remittances fell.","locator":"opening paragraph and reserve paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting replicated by a major Bangladeshi outlet with concrete macro figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 macro shock and response","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the Hasina government's macroeconomic position and response, not necessarily a personally directed policy error.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes Bangladesh had been one of the world's fastest-growing economies for years, so the 2022 deterioration sits against a strong prior trend."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Structural Reforms are Essential to Sustain Growth & Poverty Reduction in Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2023-10-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2023/10/05/structural-reforms-are-essential-to-sustain-growth-poverty-reduction-in-bangladesh","claim":"The World Bank said extreme poverty in Bangladesh had fallen to 5 percent in 2022 from 9 percent in 2016, with other wellbeing indicators such as infant mortality, stunting, electricity access, sanitation, and education also improving.","locator":"Story Highlights and opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank feature summarizing 2022 poverty survey results and broader welfare trends.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 welfare outcomes","ruler_attribution":"Outcome-level evidence under the Hasina administration, not direct proof of a specific policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The same feature warns that growth sustainability now requires policy adjustments because of balance-of-payments pressure, rising inflation, and financial-sector vulnerabilities."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

The evidence cluster is mixed rather than one-directional. It supports active macro-stabilization, budgeted social spending, procurement digitization, and some welfare gains, while also showing inflation, reserve pressure, household strain, and persistent corruption/governance problems.

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 25-40 documents across IMF, World Bank, Bangladesh Bank, Finance Ministry/NBR budget materials, BBS/BSS labor and expenditure releases, Transparency International Bangladesh, Prothom Alo, Reuters-syndicated reporting, and World Bank results pages.

Kept because they added non-duplicative 2022 evidence:
- IMF 2022 Article IV and the November 2022 IMF staff-level agreement for macro stabilization, reserve pressure, inflation, banking-sector risk, and reform direction.
- Bangladesh Bank governor biography for appointment/capacity context.
- World Bank April 2022 resilience credit, September 2022 country economic memorandum, and August 2022 procurement-results page for fiscal, financial-sector, trade, and governance reforms.
- FY2022-23 budget speech for explicit policy priorities, social spending, education, health, safety nets, women, and rural development.
- TIB CPI 2022 for corruption/capture.
- Prothom Alo HIES 2022 and BSS labor-force-survey reporting for household pressure and labor-market context.
- Prothom Alo/Reuters current-account reporting for 2022 macro deterioration.
- World Bank 2023 poverty update for retrospective 2022 welfare outcomes.

Rejected as duplicate, generic, weak, or not needed:
- Broad World Bank investment-climate pages that repeated the same Bangladesh reform theme without adding target-year detail beyond the procurement and country-memorandum sources.
- Generic global CPI and macro pages that did not add Bangladesh-specific 2022 evidence.
- Later IMF and World Bank follow-ups from 2023-2026 unless they clearly reported 2022 conditions retrospectively.
- Broad ILO labor and decent-work pages that were too generic compared with the BBS/BSS labor survey and IMF/World Bank macro sources.
- Repetitive Reuters or local rewrites of the same macro story when one strong version already carried the claim.
- Sources that were country-context only and not ruler-period specific enough to improve attribution.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence on Sheikh Hasina personally selecting, empowering, or constraining economic professionals remains thin. The strongest attribution is indirect through cabinet and finance-ministry authority.
- Better evidence would come from PMO/cabinet records, appointment notices, or contemporaneous reporting that directly ties 2022 economic decisions to Hasina rather than to the finance ministry or central bank.
- More granular 2022 data on real wages, underemployment, regional inequality, and sectoral household welfare would help with the distribution lenses.
- More direct audit or investigative material on corruption, bank-loan governance, and procurement enforcement in 2022 would sharpen the 5B.5 and 5B.7 judgments.
